{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["And the reason for <b>a new meal-offering</b> is that no meal-offering may be brought to the House of YHWH until this one is brought, as our Rabbis explained (Menahot 83a). And it was necessary to say \"a perpetual statute throughout your generations in all your dwellings\" regarding the prohibition of new grain (Leviticus 23:14) and regarding the cessation of labor on the Festival of Weeks (Leviticus 23:21), because it depends on the waving of the omer and on the new meal-offering — to say that even after our exile outside the Land, when there is no omer and meal-offering to be commanded in them, nevertheless the new grain is forbidden by Torah law in every place. And it was not necessary to mention this regarding the Day of Remembrance, and likewise regarding the Festival of Sukkot (Leviticus 23:41) it did not mention \"in all your dwellings,\" but it did mention it regarding the Day of Atonement (Leviticus 23:31), because since it tied our atonement to the offerings, as it mentioned in the section of Acharei Mot, it states here that its prohibition applies in every place, for Yom Kippur atones for you through affliction and cessation of labor, and the offerings do not hold back atonement from it. And likewise it was not mentioned here regarding the Festival of Matzot, \"a perpetual statute in all your dwellings throughout your generations,\" but in the section of \"This month shall be for you\" (Exodus 12:2, verses 14, 17, 20) it did mention it, because there the matter was tied to the Paschal offering — \"upon unleavened bread and bitter herbs shall they eat it\" (Exodus 12:8) — so it was necessary to say that it shall be \"a perpetual statute in all your dwellings.\" And regarding the Sabbath it mentioned \"in all your dwellings\" (Leviticus 23:3), and the reason is because labor is permitted in the Sanctuary for the daily offerings and additional offerings, so it says that the prohibition applies in all our dwellings, for it did not say regarding it \"a perpetual statute throughout your generations in all your dwellings,\" but rather it only means to say \"in all your dwellings\" — not in the House of HaShem. And thus they said in the Mekhilta (Vayakhel 1): In your dwellings you may not kindle, but you may kindle in the House of the Sanctuary. And likewise it is stated (Leviticus 3:17): \"a perpetual statute throughout your generations in all your dwellings\" regarding the prohibition of fat and blood, for it is forbidden on account of the offerings. And thus they said in tractate Kiddushin (37a): \"Your dwellings\" — why do I need it? One might have thought, since it is written in the context of offerings, when there is an offering yes, when there is no offering no — it teaches us otherwise. And there they said: \"Your dwellings\" — why do I need it regarding the Sabbath (Leviticus 23:3)? One might have thought, since it is written in the context of the festivals, it would require sanctification by the court like the festivals — it teaches us otherwise. And the general principle is that the Torah does not mention this for all the commandments that are obligations of the body, but only because of the need of the matter. And \"from your dwellings you shall bring bread of waving\" (Leviticus 23:17) is not like \"from all your dwellings,\" but rather its meaning is \"from the land of your dwellings,\" to exclude outside the Land, as it is stated: \"When you come into the land of your dwellings which I am giving to you\" (Numbers 15:2). And thus we learned (Menahot 83a): All offerings of the congregation and of the individual may come from the Land and from outside the Land, except for the omer and the two loaves. And even according to the one who says the omer may come from outside the Land (Menahot 84a), he concedes regarding the two loaves that they may come only from the Land."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:58:32.681810891Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}